# Design decisions — retired requirements — {{ project_name }}

One line per requirement id in [`DESIGN.oracle.md`](DESIGN.oracle.md) or
[`DESIGN.md`](DESIGN.md) that a landed decision has **retired**. Append only;
nothing here is ever edited.

## Why this is a separate file

[`DESIGN.oracle.md`](DESIGN.oracle.md) is append-only, and enforced
(`.github/scripts/oracle-decisions.sh`). That is what makes an `OD-<n>` mean
something: a landed decision cannot be reworded after a plan was built on it.

The cost of that rule is that **a requirement cannot be retired in place.** The
ledger keeps every decision on its page forever, so a requirement a later
decision reversed still reads as a live requirement of the design — and
`.github/scripts/coverage.sh` counted it as one. It reported the id `NOT
PLANNED` on every run for a behaviour deliberately not being built, and the
delivery driver, which commissions planning for exactly that report's gaps,
dispatched a planner for the dead decision every cycle and could never walk past
it. [`BACKLOG.md`](BACKLOG.md) and [`escapes.md`](escapes.md) each hit the
identical problem and got their own done-log; this is the same answer for the
same reason.

The pair is the point: the ledger says what was decided and never changes; this
says which requirements those decisions have taken out of the design. Neither
can be revised to suit the other.

**Do not remove anything from [`DESIGN.oracle.md`](DESIGN.oracle.md) when you
add a line here.** A plan that cited `OD-7` must still find `OD-7` where it was.

## This file cannot retire anything on its own

A line here is a **record of a decision**, never the decision itself.
`coverage.sh` refuses in both directions:

- a `**Requirements superseded:**` id in the ledger with **no line here** is an
  error — write the line, and the report tells you so by id;
- a line here naming an id **no landed decision supersedes** is an error too.

That second half is the important one. Without it, one appended line could
delete a requirement from the design's universe — and "every requirement is
covered" would become true by emptying the design. The authority to retire a
requirement stays where it belongs: a decision in the ledger, gated, evidenced,
and naming the vision statement it leaned on.

## What is NOT recorded here, and why

**Delivery.** A requirement that has been built and merged is not written down
here, because it is not finished — a delivered requirement is one the system
must go on satisfying forever, and the day it stops is a regression. What ends
here is a requirement's *life*, not its implementation.

Delivery also needs no record, because it already has one: a plan carries
`status:` in its front matter, and `coverage.sh` reads it. Writing it a second
time by hand would create two sources for one fact that can disagree — which is
the shape of nearly every defect this project has logged.

## Format

One line each, and the id in backticks exactly as the design spells it:

    - `R<n>` — retired by `OD-<n>`, replaced by `R<m>` — YYYY-MM-DD — why it went
    - `R<n>` — retired by `OD-<n>`, no replacement — YYYY-MM-DD — why it went

Say which decision retired it. An id here with no decision behind it is a claim,
and the gate above refuses one.

